## Answer

For a CRLF `LineTerminator`, `without_terminator` strips the two-byte `\r\n` only when it is the exact suffix. Otherwise it returns the input unchanged.

**Source** (`crates/searcher/src/lines.rs:117-126`):
```rust
let line_term = line_term.as_bytes();
let start = bytes.len().saturating_sub(line_term.len());
if bytes.get(start..) == Some(line_term) {
    return &bytes[..bytes.len() - line_term.len()];
}
bytes
```

`as_bytes` for CRLF returns `&[b'\r', b'\n']` (`crates/matcher/src/lib.rs:250-256`), so `line_term.len()` is 2.

- **Input ending in CRLF** (e.g. `b"abc\r\n"`): the last two bytes equal `\r\n`, so the function returns `&bytes[..len-2]`, i.e. `b"abc"`.
- **Input ending in a lone LF** (e.g. `b"abc\n"`): the last two bytes are `c\n`, which is not `\r\n`, so the check fails and the function returns the full input `b"abc\n"` unchanged. The lone `\n` is not stripped.

**Underflow on short input:** the code never subtracts from a possibly-small length before checking. `start` is computed with `saturating_sub`: `bytes.len().saturating_sub(2)` clamps to `0` when the input is shorter than 2 bytes. `bytes.get(start..)` then returns the whole slice (for len < 2) or `None` only if `start > len`, which cannot happen here. For a 1-byte input such as `b"\n"`, `start = 0`, the slice `b"\n"` is compared against `b"\r\n"`, fails, and the input is returned unchanged. The subtraction `bytes.len() - line_term.len()` on the strip path only runs after the equality check has confirmed `len >= 2`, so it cannot underflow. `get` (rather than indexing) also avoids a panic on out-of-range slices.

